## Summary
`openclaw onboard` / `openclaw configure` walk a plugin's `uiHints` fields and write each answered value into config via `setPathCreateStrict`. A plugin can declare a `uiHints` field key containing `__proto__` (a documented, public plugin-manifest surface that is copied through unfiltered). When the operator answers that prompt, the write lands on the shared `Object.prototype` instead of the operator's config, polluting every plain object in the process.

## Root cause
`src/secrets/path-utils.ts` `setPathCreateStrict` walks the dotted path segments and materializes/writes containers with plain bracket assignment, with no blocked-key filtering. Every other key-ingestion site in the repo guards with `isBlockedObjectKey` (`src/infra/prototype-keys.ts`); this one did not.

before:
```ts
export function setPathCreateStrict(
  root: Record<string, unknown>,
  segments: string[],
  value: unknown,
): boolean {
  if (segments.length === 0) {
    throw new Error("Target path is empty.");
  }
  let cursor: unknown = root;
  let changed = false;
```

A segment of `__proto__` makes `cursor = cursor[segment]` resolve to the real `Object.prototype`, and the leaf assignment then writes onto that shared prototype. The wizard reaches this because `src/plugins/manifest.ts` copies `raw.uiHints` through unfiltered and `src/wizard/setup.plugin-config.ts` calls `setPathCreateStrict(updatedConfig, toPathSegments(key), value)`, where `toPathSegments` is a plain `key.split(".")`.

## Fix
Reject prototype-polluting segments up front, mirroring the guard already used at every sibling ingestion site (`isBlockedObjectKey`, and the same pre-validation shape used by `src/cli/config-cli.ts` `validatePathSegments`).

after:
```ts
  if (segments.length === 0) {
    throw new Error("Target path is empty.");
  }
  for (const segment of segments) {
    if (isBlockedObjectKey(segment)) {
      throw new Error(`Refusing to write prototype-polluting path segment "${segment}".`);
    }
  }
  let cursor: unknown = root;
  let changed = false;
```

## Why this is the right boundary
`setPathCreateStrict` is the single shared mutation primitive that every writer routes through (the plugin wizard and all `src/secrets/apply.ts` callers), so one guard here closes the vector for every caller rather than patching only the wizard path. The sibling primitives in the same file do not need the guard: `setPathExistingStrict` gates every parent and the leaf on `Object.hasOwn`, so an inherited `__proto__`/`constructor` segment throws "does not exist" before any write; `deletePathStrict` only removes own keys and cannot pollute; `getPath` is read-only. Existing behavior is preserved: `__proto__`, `constructor`, and `prototype` are never valid config field names, and numeric array-index segments are never blocked keys.

## Verification
- `PATH=/opt/node/bin:$PATH node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/secrets/path-utils.test.ts` -> 9 passed. The new case `setPathCreateStrict rejects prototype-polluting segments` fails on pristine main (`× ... 1 failed`) and passes with the patch.
- `node scripts/run-oxlint.mjs src/secrets/path-utils.ts src/secrets/path-utils.test.ts` -> clean.
- `oxfmt --check src/secrets/path-utils.ts src/secrets/path-utils.test.ts` -> clean.
- `node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p tsconfig.core.json` and `-p test/tsconfig/tsconfig.core.test.json` -> clean.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: a plugin `uiHints` field key containing `__proto__` makes the onboarding wizard write an operator's answer onto the shared `Object.prototype`, polluting every plain object in the process.
Real environment tested: drove the real exported `setPathCreateStrict` from `src/secrets/path-utils.ts` with the exact segments the wizard computes from a manifest `uiHints` key (`"__proto__.polluted".split(".")`), on pristine main 124119a898 and on the patched tree. The production function and the real global object prototype stayed real; nothing was mocked.
Exact steps or command run after this patch: compute `segments = "__proto__.polluted".split(".").filter(Boolean)`, call `setPathCreateStrict({}, segments, "yes")`, then read `({}).polluted` on a fresh unrelated object.
Evidence after fix:
```
# BEFORE (pristine main 124119a898)
uiHints field key: "__proto__.polluted"
segments passed to setPathCreateStrict: ["__proto__","polluted"]
threw: (no error)
updatedConfig (the intended target): {}
({}).polluted before: undefined
({}).polluted after:  yes
# AFTER (this patch, identical inputs)
uiHints field key: "__proto__.polluted"
segments passed to setPathCreateStrict: ["__proto__","polluted"]
threw: Refusing to write prototype-polluting path segment "__proto__".
updatedConfig (the intended target): {}
({}).polluted before: undefined
({}).polluted after:  undefined
```
Observed result after fix: on pristine main the intended target stays `{}` while an unrelated fresh object gains `polluted: "yes"`, proving the write landed on the shared prototype; with the patch the call is refused and the unrelated object stays clean.
What was not tested: no live end-to-end `openclaw onboard` interactive session was driven (the wizard's segment computation and the shared mutation primitive were exercised directly instead); full build not run.
